; result: a computation that may fail, as a value (spec/errors.md §3, ADR 0040).
;
;   (use result)
;   (case (result.ok 3) (result.ok v) v (result.err e) 0)
;   (try (result.ok v) e body)        ; continue on ok, return any err unchanged
;
; THE ALGEBRA. (result T E) is the coproduct T + E: exactly one of a T or an E,
; eliminated by `case`, the copairing [f, g]. Marked (success ok) it is a model
; of the exception monad A + R with E = R: return is `ok`, and `try` is its bind.
; Marked (relevant), a result may not be dropped: the structural rule of
; weakening is denied for it, and (ignore r) writes a discard down.
;
; IT IS A LIBRARY, not part of the language. The compiler knows the two roles,
; `success` and `relevant`, and reads them off whatever variant declares them;
; `option` in `lang` marks `some`, and a program's own flat sum may mark its own.
;
; err-map IS FUNCTORIALITY in E. result is a bifunctor (T, E) ↦ T + E, and
; err-map f = id + f moves a result between error types where one module's
; errors meet another's:
;
;   err-map id = id                  err-map (g ∘ f) = err-map g ∘ err-map f
(module result)

(export ok err err-map)

(variant (result T E) (ok T) (err E) (success ok) (relevant))

(def err-map (f r)
  (case r
    (ok v)  (ok v)
    (err e) (err (f e))))
